{'slug': 'how-uk-shoe-stores-cut-size-returns-with-ar', 'title': 'How UK Shoe Stores Cut Size Returns with AR (No App Needed)', 'date': 'October 9, 2026', 'description': 'A UK shoe store loses money every time a customer guesses the wrong size. Here is how AR try-on fixes the fit guess: what it costs, how it goes live, and the questions to ask.', 'body': '<figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/how-uk-shoe-stores-cut-size-returns-with-ar/images/hero.jpg" alt="A shopper in her living room using her phone to see a chelsea boot in AR try-on on her own foot" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Seeing the shoe on your own foot, at true size, before you buy.</figcaption></figure><p>Picture a shoe store owner in Leeds. She sells leather boots and trainers from one shop and a website. Every Monday, the post brings back boxes. Boots that did not fit. Trainers a half size too small. Each return costs her the postage, the inspection time, and a pair that comes back scuffed and no longer sellable at full price.</p><p>She is not alone, and the cause is not her shoes. It is the size guess. Online, a customer picks a size from a chart, hopes the brand runs true, and waits. When it does not fit, the store pays.</p><h2>Why size charts do not fix it</h2><p>UK shoe sizes add their own confusion. A size 7 in one brand fits like a 6 in another. A wide foot in a standard last feels a half size down. Size charts tell the customer the numbers are right, but they cannot tell her how the shoe sits on her own foot. So she orders two sizes and sends one back, or she orders one and sends it back.</p><p>AR try-on answers the actual question. The customer points her phone at her feet and sees the shoe on her own foot, at true size, in the light of her own room. She checks the shape, the length, the look, before the buy button. The guess goes away.</p><figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/how-uk-shoe-stores-cut-size-returns-with-ar/images/img1.jpg" alt="Close-up of a phone showing a brown leather boot in AR try-on beside the real boot" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A true-to-size AR view next to the real boot.</figcaption></figure><h2>What AR try-on changes for the store</h2><p>Fewer size mistakes mean fewer returns. It is that direct. The customer who sees the fit before buying is the customer who keeps the box. Shopify reports that products with 3D or AR views convert about 94% better than products without. That is Shopify\'s own data, not ours, and it lines up with the mechanism: shoppers who can check the fit hesitate less and send fewer pairs back.</p><p>It also changes who buys. Customers who never order shoes online because of the fit gamble start ordering. That is new revenue, not just saved postage.</p><h2>How a small store gets it live</h2><p>There are two routes. The first is do it yourself with a viewer app. On Shopify, AR viewer apps add a 3D viewer to your product pages for a small monthly fee. But they are viewers only. You supply every 3D model yourself, usually commissioned from 3D artists at roughly $100 to $500 per product. A shoe store with 40 styles needs dozens of models. The subscription is small. The modelling bill is the project. We worked the same maths in our <a href="/blog/ar-cost-small-footwear-store-pricing">pricing breakdown for footwear stores</a>.</p><p>The second route is done for you. You send photos of your bestselling shoes and their real listed dimensions. ARQR360 builds true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views from them, hosts them, and hands you links and QR codes. The price is flat and public: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores, with the AR (Augmented Reality) views included. About seven days from photos to a live catalogue. No 3D team, no platform to learn.</p><figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/how-uk-shoe-stores-cut-size-returns-with-ar/images/img2.jpg" alt="A shopkeeper in a small UK shoe shop packing a returned shoe box at the counter" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Every returned box is postage, time, and a pair that may not resell at full price.</figcaption></figure><h2>The app question</h2><p>Ask every provider this before you sign: does the customer need to download an app? If the answer is yes, most of your customers will never use it. People do not install apps to look at a boot. Browser-based try-on opens from a link or a QR code in seconds. That is the version that actually gets used. Try it yourself first: our <a href="/corso">live footwear demo</a> opens in your phone browser, no install.</p><h2>What to check before you choose</h2><ul><li><strong>True-to-size proof:</strong> for shoes, a view even slightly off teaches the customer the tool cannot be trusted. Ask how the provider verifies scale against the real shoe.</li><li><strong>Who builds the models:</strong> on most routes the modelling bill, not the software, is the biggest cost. Ask what is included and what new-season stock costs.</li><li><strong>Total cost:</strong> add the models, the setup, and the next range. Per-model fees quietly dwarf subscriptions.</li><li><strong>Where the link lives:</strong> try-on buried in a menu nobody opens does nothing. The link belongs next to the price, where the buying decision happens.</li></ul><p>For a full tour of the options, read <a href="/blog/best-ar-tools-footwear-stores-2026">the best AR tools for footwear stores in 2026</a>.</p><h2>The cheapest way to check if it works for your store</h2><p>You do not have to commit to anything to find out. Send a photo of your bestselling shoe and we will build you one true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view free. Put it on your own foot, check it against the real shoe, and see if your customers would use it. If it does not convince you, you have spent nothing. Try the <a href="/corso">live footwear demo</a> first: it opens in your phone browser, no install.</p><div class="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Do my customers need an app to try shoes on?</h3><p>No. With ARQR360, each shoe gets a link and a QR code that opens the try-on in the phone browser. Try the <a href="/corso">live footwear demo</a> on your own phone. Some platforms run try-on inside apps, so ask before you sign.</p><h3>What does AR cost a small UK shoe store?</h3><p>It depends on who builds the models, which is the biggest cost on most routes. ARQR360 is flat and public: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores, with the AR (Augmented Reality) views included. The first view is free.</p><h3>How long before my shoes are live in AR?</h3><p>With ARQR360, about seven days from your photos arriving. On self-serve routes it depends on model turnaround plus your own integration work. Ask for a written timeline for your catalogue size before you commit.</p></div>'},
{"slug": "arqr360-vs-sayduck-rug-stores", "title": "ARQR360 vs Sayduck: What a Rug Store Actually Gets from Each", "date": "October 9, 2026", "description": "Comparing Sayduck and ARQR360 for your rug store? A fair look at price, setup effort, true-to-size accuracy, and who builds the 3D models, written for independent retailers.", "body": """<figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/arqr360-vs-sayduck-rug-stores/images/hero.jpg" alt="A patterned area rug laid true to size under a coffee table in a warm living room" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The question every rug buyer asks: what will this rug do to my room?</figcaption></figure><p>Picture a rug store owner in Manchester. She sells Persian-style and modern rugs from one shop and a website. Her customers love the patterns online but hesitate at the same point. They cannot tell if the rug will fit their living room. She starts looking into AR and lands on two names. Sayduck, the 3D viewer company behind many design brands. And ARQR360. Both promise AR. They are built for very different buyers. Here is the honest version.</p><h2>What Sayduck is</h2><p>Sayduck is a 3D product visualisation company from Helsinki. Its platform gives online stores a 3D viewer, a product configurator, and an AR preview that runs in the phone browser with no app. Shoppers can spin the product, change colours and materials, and then place it in their own room at real scale.</p><p>For Shopify stores there is a Sayduck app that syncs the catalogue with no code. Prices and stock update with each configuration. The company also runs a 3D creation service that builds the models from your product images when you do not have them.</p><figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/arqr360-vs-sayduck-rug-stores/images/img1.jpg" alt="A shopper holding her phone to see a rug at true size on her own living room floor in AR" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Seeing the rug on your own floor, at its real size, before you buy.</figcaption></figure><h2>What ARQR360 is</h2><p>ARQR360 is a done-for-you AR (Augmented Reality) catalogue for independent rug retailers. You send photos of your bestselling rugs and we build the true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views from them, host them, and hand you links and QR codes that open the AR view in your customer's phone browser. No app. No platform to learn. No 3D team.</p><p>The audience is different. ARQR360 is built for stores that sell finished products off the shelf, not brands running configurator projects across a catalogue.</p><h2>Price: platform subscription vs one flat rate</h2><p>Sayduck does not publish one fixed price list. Software directories list monthly tiers in the area of $29 to $99, and their 3D creation service is priced per model. Those figures move, so the only reliable number is the one you get from them in writing. The subscription buys the platform: the viewer, the configurator, the AR preview. The 3D models your products need are a separate line.</p><p>ARQR360 publishes its price: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, locked for life for the first 50 stores. The AR (Augmented Reality) views are included. No product cap, no per-model fee, no per-view fee.</p><h2>Setup effort: syncing a catalogue vs sending photos</h2><p>A Sayduck project starts with your catalogue. If you run Shopify, the app syncs products and the setup is no-code. But AR needs a 3D model of each rug. If you do not have models, someone has to build them: their creation service or your own supplier. For rugs the configurator is often the part you use least. A rug is a fixed pattern at a fixed size. What the customer needs is the one true-to-size view on their own floor.</p><p>With ARQR360 the project is: send photos, get a working AR catalogue back in about seven days. The links go on your product pages. The QR codes go in the shop. There is nothing to integrate and nothing to maintain.</p><h2>True-to-size accuracy</h2><p>Both platforms show products at real dimensions. The honest difference is not the viewer. It is what you go through to get each rug live, and what you pay every year to keep it there. For a store whose problem is customers guessing at size, a true-to-size AR view that ships this month beats a full platform that ships next quarter.</p><p>Shopify reports that products with 3D or AR views convert about 94% better than products without. That is Shopify's own data, not ours. Whichever route you take, giving shoppers a true-to-size view beats a flat photo.</p><figure class="postimg"><img src="/blog/arqr360-vs-sayduck-rug-stores/images/img2.jpg" alt="A phone showing a 3D rug viewer beside a real rug hanging in a rug store" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The viewer shows what the model says. The model has to be true to size.</figcaption></figure><h2>Who each one fits</h2><p><strong>Sayduck makes sense if:</strong></p><ul><li>You are a brand with products customers configure: colours, fabrics, sizes</li><li>You want the configurator, not just the AR view</li><li>You already have 3D models, or budget for per-model creation</li><li>You run Shopify and want a no-code catalogue sync</li></ul><p><strong>ARQR360 is designed for stores like yours if:</strong></p><ul><li>You are an independent rug retailer or showroom</li><li>Your rugs are fixed products, not configurable</li><li>You hold no 3D files and want the whole thing handled</li><li>You use Shopify, WooCommerce, or a simple website</li><li>You need one public price with no surprises</li></ul><h2>The question to ask both vendors</h2><p>Before you sign anything, ask both of us the same four questions in writing: what is the full annual cost including every model, who builds the 3D models and from what source material, what happens to the models if you end the contract, and does the customer need to download an app. The answers will tell you which tool fits your store faster than any feature list.</p><p>For the returns side of the story, read <a href="/blog/how-rug-stores-cut-size-returns-with-ar">how rug stores cut size returns with AR</a>.</p><h2>The cheapest way to check if AR works for your store</h2><p>You do not have to commit to anything to find out. Send a photo of your bestselling rug and we will build you one true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) view free. Put it on your own floor, walk around it, check the size with your own eyes, and decide with the thing in front of you. If it does not convince you, you have spent nothing. Try the <a href="/terra">live rug demo</a> first: it opens in your phone browser, no install.</p><div class="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How much does Sayduck cost?</h3><p>Sayduck does not publish one fixed price. Software directories list monthly tiers in the area of $29 to $99, with 3D model creation priced per model. Get their current quote in writing before you decide. ARQR360 publishes its full price: $199 one-time setup and $19 a month, AR (Augmented Reality) views included.</p><h3>Who builds the 3D models?</h3><p>With Sayduck, you supply the 3D models or use their 3D creation service. With ARQR360, we build the true-to-size AR (Augmented Reality) views from your product photos and the real listed dimensions. Your first view is free so you can check the quality before spending anything.</p><h3>Do my customers need to download an app?</h3><p>No, not with either platform. Sayduck's AR preview runs in the phone browser, and ARQR360 links and QR codes open the same way. Try the <a href="/terra">live rug demo</a> on your own phone and see.</p></div>"""},

